# Commands and results

- `.local/verify-oidc.ps1`: passed. Trusted Publisher workflow contract, minimal permissions, npm `11.18.0`, five build targets, provenance, absence of token secrets, package identity, and `actionlint` all passed.
- `go test ./... -count=1`: passed for all Go packages.
- `go vet ./...`: passed.
- `npm test`: passed, 3 tests.
- `.local/verify-package.ps1`: passed; five supported platform binaries built and the npm dry-run package contained all expected binaries.
- `comet native check npm-trusted-publishing`: passed; 2 implementation files scanned, 0 issues.

# Skipped checks

- No production Git tag was pushed and no GitHub Release or second npm version was published. The change explicitly excludes a real production release; the npm Trusted Publisher binding was verified from the configured package UI and the workflow contract.

# Spec consistency

The workflow keeps global `contents: read`, grants `contents: write` and `id-token: write` only to the publish job, installs and logs npm `11.18.0`, retains all existing tests/builds/checksums/package checks, publishes with public access and provenance, and contains no `NPM_TOKEN` or `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` reference.

# Known limitations and risks

- The Trusted Publisher repository/workflow binding is npm account state outside the repository. If that UI configuration is renamed or removed, OIDC publication will fail while tests and builds can still pass.
- `0.0.1` is already immutable in npm. The next end-to-end production workflow run must use a higher tag such as `v0.0.2`.

# Conclusion

Pass. All 11 acceptance items have fresh typed evidence, project tests and five-platform builds pass, and the release workflow is ready to publish through npm Trusted Publisher OIDC without a long-lived npm token.
